@id     02_conditions
@title  Conditions
@topic  if/else
@lang   c
@show   values

@theory
scanf reads input. &n means "the address of n", so scanf can store the value there.

    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n > 10) {
        printf("big\n");
    } else {
        printf("small\n");
    }

You can chain checks with else if.

Your task: read one integer and print
positive  if it is greater than 0
negative  if it is less than 0
zero      if it is 0

@read
## Making a choice
Programs often have to choose: do this, or do that. In C, `if` checks a condition. When the condition is true, the first block runs. When it is false, the `else` block runs. Never both.

    int x = 7;
    if (x >= 10) {
        printf("large\n");
    } else {
        printf("small\n");      // this one runs: 7 >= 10 is false
    }

## Comparing values
- `a == b` is a equal to b (two = signs!)
- `a != b` is a not equal to b
- `a < b`, `a > b`, `a <= b`, `a >= b`

A single `=` is not a comparison. `x = 5` puts 5 into x; `x == 5` asks "is x equal to 5?". Mixing them up is a classic bug.

## More than two choices
`else if` checks another condition when the first one was false. C checks them from top to bottom and runs only the first block whose condition is true.

    if (score >= 90) {
        printf("A\n");
    } else if (score >= 50) {
        printf("Pass\n");
    } else {
        printf("Fail\n");
    }

## Read more
Beej's Guide to C Programming, chapter 3 "Variables and Statements", section on flow control (if and else) — free at beej.us/guide/bgc

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    // your code here

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n > 0) printf("positive\n");
    else if (n < 0) printf("negative\n");
    else printf("zero\n");
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
5
@expected
positive

@test
@input
-3
@expected
negative

@hidden_test
@input
0
@expected
zero

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step explore
@title  Only one branch runs
@scene  condition
@text
Move the slider and watch which block of the if runs.
The if checks x >= 10. When it is true the first block runs, otherwise the else block runs. Never both.

@step watch
@title  Watch an if
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int temperature = 25;
    if (temperature > 20) {
        printf("Warm\n");
    } else {
        printf("Cold\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch which printf runs.
On the if line the program checks temperature > 20. The sentence under the picture tells you if it was true.

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int temperature = 15;
    if (temperature > 20) {
        printf("Warm\n");
    } else {
        printf("Cold\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@text
What does the program print now that the temperature is 15?

@step choose
@title  Compare or assign?
@code
int x = 5;
if (/* which condition? */) {
    printf("x is five\n");
}
@text
Which condition checks whether x is equal to 5?
@option if (x = 5)
@option if (x == 5)
@option if (x != 5)
@option if (5 = x)
@correct 2
@answer
== compares two values. A single = puts a value into a variable, and != means "not equal".

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix the if line so that a score of exactly 50 passes.
A score of 50 or more should pass, but right now 50 gets fail. Use Debug my code to watch it.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int score;
    scanf("%d", &score);
    if (score > 50) {
        printf("pass\n");
    } else {
        printf("fail\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@editable 6-6
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int score;
    scanf("%d", &score);
    if (score >= 50) {
        printf("pass\n");
    } else {
        printf("fail\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@test
@input
50
@expected
pass
@test
@input
70
@expected
pass
@hidden_test
@input
49
@expected
fail

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
Why does only one of the two printf lines run in an if / else?
@rubric
condition; true; false; else
@answer
The if checks a condition. When it is true, the first block runs and the else block is
skipped. When it is false, only the else block runs.

@step practice
@title  Practice
